#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105511 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a087e7bc-4e74-584d-af3b-bf6bee4b75ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Their Components"}] :question/id #uuid "3d38f3e0-4c27-5ac6-ac16-9c8f57e0000e" :question/math-academy-id "e-4327" :question/problem "Find the angle between $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 1,5,-2 \\rangle$. Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\mid a \\mid \\mid b \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{a_{x}b_{x} + a_{y}b_{y} + a_{z}b_{z}}{\\sqrt{a_{x}^{2} + a_{y}^{2} + a_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{b_{x}^{2} + b_{y}^{2} + b_{z}^{2}}} \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot 1 + (-1) \\cdot 5 + 1 \\cdot (-2))/(\\sqrt{2^{2} + (-1)^{2} + 1^{2}}\\sqrt{1^{2} + 5^{2} + (-2)^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{6} \\cdot \\sqrt{30}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-5}{\\sqrt{6} \\cdot \\sqrt{6} \\cdot \\sqrt{5}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{6} \\right) \\approx 111.9^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105512 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/id #uuid "d6d1be58-7321-50ca-a47d-5e80d55277c3" :question/math-academy-id "e-4328" :question/problem "Find $\\angle BAC$ given the points $A(2,-1,-5)$, $B(-1,0,-4)$, and $C(5,1,1)$. Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\na = ⟨2,-1,-5⟩, b = ⟨ - 1, 0,-4⟩, c = ⟨5, 1, 1⟩\n$$\n\nThe triangle $\\triangle ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote\n\n$$\n\\angle BAC = \\theta\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1278/Source/Images/3326328c02627ffa40dbcdf7cb16cc96.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB^(→) &= b - a \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 1, 0,-4⟩ - ⟨2,-1,-5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - 3, 1, 1⟩ \\\\\nAC^(→) &= c - a \\\\\n&= ⟨5, 1, 1⟩ - ⟨2,-1,-5⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨3, 2, 6⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= (AB^(→) \\cdot AC^(→))/(\\mid AB^(→) \\mid \\mid AC^(→) \\mid) \\\\\n&= (-3 \\cdot 3 + 1 \\cdot 2 + 1 \\cdot 6)/(\\sqrt{(-3)^{2} + 1^{2} + 1^{2}}\\sqrt{3^{2} + 2^{2} + 6^{2}}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-1}{\\sqrt{11}\\sqrt{49}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{7\\sqrt{11}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left(-\\dfrac{1}{7\\sqrt{11}} \\right) \\approx 92.5^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105529 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a418d9aa-d74d-58b1-a4c9-819f63593d50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given the Value of Their Dot Product"}] :question/id #uuid "fb2df468-d70d-5300-8860-0cb729cb13c5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5112" :question/problem "Given that $\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=-4$, find the angle between $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle t, \\, -2 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\langle 6, \\, t \\rangle$. Round your answer to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "First, since\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a}\\cdot \\mathbf{b}=-4\n$$\n\n, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na \\cdot b &= -4 \\\\\nt \\cdot 6 + (-2) \\cdot t &= -4 \\\\\n6t - 2t &= -4 \\\\\n4t &= -4 \\\\\nt &= -1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\na = ⟨ - 1,-2⟩, b = ⟨6,-1⟩\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\cos θ &= \\frac{a \\cdot b}{\\mid a \\mid \\mid b \\mid} \\\\\n&= \\frac{a_{x}b_{x} + a_{y}b_{y}}{\\sqrt{a_{x}^{2} + a_{y}^{2}}\\sqrt{b_{x}^{2} + b_{y}^{2}}} \\\\\n&= (-4)/(\\sqrt{(-1)^{2} + (-2)^{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt{6^{2} + (-1)^{2}}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{5} \\cdot \\sqrt{37}} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{185}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos \\left(-\\dfrac{4}{\\sqrt{185}} \\right) \\approx 107.1^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb1e08c5-8191-51cb-828c-4e1bf4d18edb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269075} {:answer/id #uuid "c48d61db-8a7b-58a9-baa3-b45f0e2b2e24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{77}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269076} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe43b6a-8e20-5aa9-b47c-1e0b409544b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{86}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269077} {:answer/id #uuid "a8481d0e-1847-5899-a263-24206ff622f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{91}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269078} {:answer/id #uuid "f68369eb-d5b2-597a-8ad3-4e02ef54893c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fe43b6a-8e20-5aa9-b47c-1e0b409544b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{86}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269077} :answer-field/id #uuid "4237779a-fb1d-5aae-9935-ca22c819fe5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "16a09fd3-be95-51b4-b0ac-0c24039ae9ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-101511" :question/problem "Find $∠BAC$ given the points $A(2,-1,1)$, $B(3,1,-2)$, and $C(-1,1,1).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2,-1,1⟩,\\,b=⟨3,1,-2⟩,\\,c=⟨-1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964292/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨3,1,-2⟩-⟨2,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,1⟩-⟨2,-1,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,2,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 0}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{14}\\cdot \\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{182}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{182}})≈{86}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a418d9aa-d74d-58b1-a4c9-819f63593d50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given the Value of Their Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33d889d7-3053-5698-b65c-fe5ae9aa07f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75.22}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269089} {:answer/id #uuid "bf85c120-7483-5553-bdcd-a44069470789" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{78.55}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269090} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6a7436-5d40-5ead-afe9-8fc3f33b83ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{70.99}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269091} {:answer/id #uuid "08336714-fd00-56fc-9883-ef766d26fd89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85.33}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269092} {:answer/id #uuid "7934f66e-abaa-5481-ab13-a79e21a04132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{80.44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7934f66e-abaa-5481-ab13-a79e21a04132" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{80.44}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269093} :answer-field/id #uuid "69c601c3-8547-5f8b-8af3-b0698702489b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8fc7cf1b-c116-59a4-83f2-94ca1412ae63" :question/math-academy-id "q-101520" :question/problem "Consider the points $D(t,5,3)$, $E({t}^{2},2,t)$ and $F(t+3,{t}^{2},1).$ Find $∠EDF$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{DF}=6.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $D$, $E$, $F$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d=⟨t,\\,5,\\,3⟩,\\,e=⟨{t}^{2},\\,2,\\,t⟩,\\,f=⟨t+3,\\,{t}^{2},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△DEF$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠EDF=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964292/assets/c9cfb3e84e6f76d243125f2a09246b2a1300a0134e71359bb2b528082f064f93.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =e-d \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2},\\,2,\\,t⟩-⟨t,\\,5,\\,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-t,\\,-3,\\,t-3⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =f-d \\\\  & =⟨t+3,\\,{t}^{2},\\,1⟩-⟨t,\\,5,\\,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,\\,{t}^{2}-5,\\,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{DF}=6$, we can solve for $t$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =6 \\\\ ({t}^{2}-t)\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot ({t}^{2}-5)+(t-3)\\cdot (-2) & =6 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-3t-3{t}^{2}+15-2t+6 & =6 \\\\ -5t+21 & =6 \\\\ -5t & =-15 \\\\ t & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DE} & =⟨{3}^{2}-3,-3,3-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,-3,0⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{DF} & =⟨3,{3}^{2}-5,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,4,-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{DE}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{DF}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{DE}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{DF}}{|\\overset{\\to }{DE}||\\overset{\\to }{DF}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{{6}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\sqrt{29}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{145}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{145}})≈{80.44}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186279158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e66664f5-380e-5745-be2c-90ad323f2dfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{46}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279160} {:answer/id #uuid "0714c6d5-fa2e-5d2a-b410-9cc7f6f50f9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{41}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279161} {:answer/id #uuid "726f5315-901e-5c3f-a2a2-290b90bc03b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{38}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279162} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc7251c-e514-5ea7-bf1a-270ab49b97f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279163} {:answer/id #uuid "0adb7bb9-01cb-5473-8b8b-f7b52effa7b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0714c6d5-fa2e-5d2a-b410-9cc7f6f50f9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{41}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279161} :answer-field/id #uuid "6f5b0741-3fca-5a95-8d71-75222a5d3195" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2793518d-ad57-5b32-92b4-c966424db7d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-124813" :question/problem "Find $∠BAC$ given the points $A(2,-1,2)$, $B(3,-5,1)$, and $C(1,-4,-1).$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A$, $B$, $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨2,-1,2⟩,\\,b=⟨3,-5,1⟩,\\,c=⟨1,-4,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨3,-5,1⟩-⟨2,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-4,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨1,-4,-1⟩-⟨2,-1,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,-3,-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (-1)+(-4)\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot (-3)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-3)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{\\sqrt{18}\\cdot \\sqrt{19}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3\\sqrt{38}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{14}{3\\sqrt{38}})≈{41}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106031 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a087e7bc-4e74-584d-af3b-bf6bee4b75ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Their Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "981c0757-fe53-59c1-96f1-1e7b765c291c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "35^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102424} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f14e2e-cbcf-5479-8070-9329dd4c6100" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102425} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e95d0e-98cd-57c8-91db-15c16b7abcf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102426} {:answer/id #uuid "04911650-f394-5f92-89c4-a8ad3def4fe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "78^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102427} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac0780e-b0e2-5eaf-baa3-7b52daaa8a34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102428}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8e95d0e-98cd-57c8-91db-15c16b7abcf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102426} :answer-field/id #uuid "31367477-0f1a-5ca5-9bf8-a56c2f82c41e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102423}] :question/id #uuid "fefdea41-9260-5cb7-a494-74f9e1519448" :question/math-academy-id "q-19035" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFind the angle between $a = ⟨3, 2, 1⟩$ and $b = ⟨1, 2, 3⟩$. Round your answer to the nearest degree." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269108 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a087e7bc-4e74-584d-af3b-bf6bee4b75ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Their Components"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8afbfb09-b22c-5fb4-ad63-9b272b5b0f58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{74.66}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269110} {:answer/id #uuid "8963ce6a-0bfd-5fd2-b165-d792ada55b90" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{82.28}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269111} {:answer/id #uuid "bc6a8900-1583-5035-b877-a331a777d7a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79.76}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269112} {:answer/id #uuid "5826b01a-a2bb-59f5-97f6-55db95fe21e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{94.32}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269113} {:answer/id #uuid "6745ac4a-89a9-5753-84d7-9c7d0584dc63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85.82}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6745ac4a-89a9-5753-84d7-9c7d0584dc63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85.82}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269114} :answer-field/id #uuid "a854aaae-2f37-5309-b178-44153a0fe3f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269109}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "de2bc7db-4647-58f0-bdd4-89b0098b6ec1" :question/math-academy-id "q-19036" :question/problem "Find the angle between $a=⟨4,-3,2⟩$ and $b=⟨3,4,1⟩.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "Rearranging the dot product formula, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{a\\cdot b}{|\\,a\\,||\\,b\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{a}_{x}^{2}+{a}_{y}^{2}+{a}_{z}^{2}}\\sqrt{{b}_{x}^{2}+{b}_{y}^{2}+{b}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{29}\\cdot \\sqrt{26}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{754}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{754}})≈{85.82}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aca6bd26-6933-59e2-8800-0702ffaaee5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102430} {:answer/id #uuid "b59b3837-6fdf-509f-b5ae-fc2925a1d015" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102431} {:answer/id #uuid "bd485a1b-1304-5ab4-ac9e-8d4a2f9d2b36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102432} {:answer/id #uuid "08d4a2e8-a01f-5019-a296-05c07d911af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102433} {:answer/id #uuid "673c1a77-a57c-5bbd-8e96-8088500a00a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102434}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08d4a2e8-a01f-5019-a296-05c07d911af5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102433} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2546906-9052-54aa-8870-1c81e7a0a86b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f6f80f82-94f9-5bde-ac4d-44929ef66109" :question/math-academy-id "q-19110" :question/problem "Find $∠QPR$ given the points $P(0,4,5)$, $Q(1,5,4)$, and $R(2,3,6).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $P$, $Q$, $R$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p=⟨0,4,5⟩,\\,q=⟨1,5,4⟩,\\,r=⟨2,3,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $△PQR$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠QPR=\\theta .$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978202/assets/3003f0cb329a8f8ef9bc315c70df4773ac1f0c54843dea1208c68bd0f6227bd1.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =q-p \\\\  & =⟨1,5,4⟩-⟨0,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{PR} & =r-p \\\\  & =⟨2,3,6⟩-⟨0,4,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{PR}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{PR}}{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}||\\overset{\\to }{PR}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 1}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0)={90}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106033 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7b1b8dc-bb50-59b4-8716-bdd6fbeada4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given Three Points"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "939952d1-0149-556b-a88e-f0b50063c995" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "102^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102436} {:answer/id #uuid "d96db57b-61cd-5adc-9c38-4cb9988be22f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102437} {:answer/id #uuid "0341b012-c894-5a3e-8faf-ee417a81b04f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102438} {:answer/id #uuid "10dd6f82-5f13-59a8-9a02-f812afe863b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "51^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102439} {:answer/id #uuid "7bed2b44-02ab-5ef6-9f67-769273a601a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "10dd6f82-5f13-59a8-9a02-f812afe863b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "51^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102439} :answer-field/id #uuid "9777b9d4-0c7d-5416-9108-e56715ea430c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102435}] :question/id #uuid "adc865e5-b770-53c4-be9e-d693f63bd5dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-19112" :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nFind $∠BAC$ given the points $A(3,-3, 1)$, $B(5,-3, 3)$, and $C(3,-2, 3)$. Round your answer to the nearest degree." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a418d9aa-d74d-58b1-a4c9-819f63593d50" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Angle Between Two Vectors Given the Value of Their Dot Product"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb9d024a-612a-5b88-b561-2eca5dd12c41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{65}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269152} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee2a05a-ca70-5223-ba9e-36cd6fa08e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269153} {:answer/id #uuid "6eaac85c-277b-51f1-baf6-a4d8434347b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{82}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269154} {:answer/id #uuid "a4eef7ee-ce27-568c-aa25-74f1486ec107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{58}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269155} {:answer/id #uuid "3f8ec3a5-7e51-5c63-9ee0-131bcdf218d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269156}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f8ec3a5-7e51-5c63-9ee0-131bcdf218d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{75}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269156} :answer-field/id #uuid "89db74db-3d63-59f5-b41d-544e3f33dabd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "61191758-28d5-55a6-95f0-ed679272a908" :question/math-academy-id "q-77164" :question/problem "Consider the points $A(3t,t,4),$ $B(3t,4t,3)$ and $C(5t,t+1,5).$ Find $∠BAC$ given that $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=2.$ Round your answer to the nearest degree." :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a=⟨3t,\\,t,\\,4⟩,\\,b=⟨3t,\\,4t,\\,3⟩,\\,c=⟨5t,\\,t+1,\\,5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe triangle $△ABC$ is shown (schematically) in the diagram below, where we denote $∠BAC=\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964292/assets/285b8343208079181ac6036cf9ca16abc974d59b9262878ed40c0da9af5683d0.png)\n\nFirst, we find the components of $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =b-a \\\\  & =⟨3t,\\,4t,\\,3⟩-⟨3t,\\,t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,3t,\\,-1⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =c-a \\\\  & =⟨5t,\\,t+1,\\,5⟩-⟨3t,\\,t,\\,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}=2$, we can solve for $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =2 \\\\ 0\\cdot 2t+3t\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 1 & =2 \\\\ 3t-1 & =2 \\\\ 3t & =3 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨0,3,-1⟩, \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =⟨2,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the cosine of the angle between $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}\\cdot \\overset{\\to }{AC}}{|\\overset{\\to }{AB}||\\overset{\\to }{AC}|} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{15}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{15}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{15}})≈{75}^{∘}.\n$$"}]}
